{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central transfer claim — that a non-zero baseline of isolated, diverse failures is a *signal* of structural independence and that a 'too quiet' redundant system is *more likely* to mask a hidden common-cause vulnerability — inverts the evidential logic the problem asks about. The problem requires identifying evidence that distinguishes genuinely independent failures from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause failures. But a steady stream of isolated failures is itself compatible with a shared latent cause that is partially triggered (e.g., a common library bug that manifests stochastically across nodes under varying load). SRC_001 treats the 'noise' as positive evidence of independence without specifying any causal test that would separate noisy-but-independent from noisy-but-shared-cause. In doing so it confuses a description of the failure pattern with evidence of its causal origin — the very conflation the problem asks to resolve.","counterexample":[{"claimed_inference":"Diverse isolated failures signal structural independence","observed_pattern":"Non-zero, diverse isolated failures across nodes","system":"Common-library bug causing intermittent isolated node crashes at low rate","true_cause":"Shared latent dependency","verdict":"False positive — SRC_001's heuristic would wrongly classify this as independent"}],"target":"1df3890ac1031ad11410dde9304809a012a9defb0c2d728236c62de276861c57"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's disanalogy — that a zero-day in a foundational decentralized protocol can wipe out the entire network simultaneously, whereas a bridge-population failure is local — undercuts its own claim rather than supporting it. The problem asks whether the decentralized network is *more* resilient to correlated failure than one large service. If the shared-structure mapping is correct that a foundational protocol bug triggers simultaneous correlated failure across all seemingly independent components, then the entire network lacks causal firebreaks against that class and collapses exactly like a monoculture. SRC_002 presents this as a disanalogy to keep the biological mapping clean, but it actually identifies a correlated-failure pathway for the decentralized system that its own condition ('causal firebreaks') does not block: a consensus-layer zero-day is a shared cause internal to the decentralized design, so the disanalogy does not weaken the biological analogy — it weakens the claim that the decentralized network is more resilient.","counterexample":[{"centralized_system":"Single large service also vulnerable to its consensus/coordination bug","claimed_by_src_002":"Disanalogy preserves the pro-decentralization claim","decentralized_system":"All independently-operated nodes share the same protocol bug and fail simultaneously","failure_mode":"Consensus-layer zero-day common to all nodes","resilience_delta":"No advantage — both fail correlatedly","verdict":"The disanalogy is a genuine shared-failure path, so the claim is weaker than stated"}],"target":"aecc64bbc2846f596c481575fe294d984b413ba8ebd1d50ef313a049a7326362"}]}